1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,696
Phase noise is how an oscillator lies to you: its frequency is right on average,

2
00:00:06,696 --> 00:00:10,863
but every individual cycle arrives a tiny bit early or a tiny bit late.

3
00:00:10,863 --> 00:00:15,265
That rapid, random wobble in phase smears what should be an infinitely thin

4
00:00:15,265 --> 00:00:19,902
spectral line into a mound with sloping "skirts" — and inside a receiver, those

5
00:00:19,902 --> 00:00:24,774
skirts are the mechanism that lets a strong signal next door raise the noise on the

6
00:00:24,774 --> 00:00:27,063
channel you're actually trying to hear.

7
00:00:27,513 --> 00:00:30,382
Picture two metronomes set to the same tempo.

8
00:00:30,382 --> 00:00:32,485
One ticks with machine precision.

9
00:00:32,485 --> 00:00:37,584
The other keeps perfect time on average — count its ticks over a minute and it's

10
00:00:37,584 --> 00:00:42,939
spot-on — but each individual tick lands slightly early or slightly late, at random.

11
00:00:42,939 --> 00:00:45,361
That second metronome has phase noise.

12
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:50,397
And notice it's a different flaw from drift: an oscillator can hold its average

13
00:00:50,397 --> 00:00:55,368
frequency beautifully over months and still jitter from cycle to cycle — or be

14
00:00:55,368 --> 00:00:59,320
rock-steady tick-to-tick while slowly wandering off frequency.

15
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,655
Short-term jitter and long-term stability are separate report cards.

16
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:06,715
The spectrum view makes the wobble visible.

17
00:01:06,715 --> 00:01:11,694
An oscillator's output is a cosine at its frequency plus a small random phase term

18
00:01:11,694 --> 00:01:13,272
that wanders continuously.

19
00:01:13,272 --> 00:01:17,947
That wander takes energy which belongs in a single line and spreads it into a

20
00:01:17,947 --> 00:01:21,772
continuous pedestal on either side of the carrier — the skirts.

21
00:01:21,772 --> 00:01:26,628
They're worst close in and fall away as you move out, eventually flattening into

22
00:01:26,628 --> 00:01:28,632
the oscillator's broadband noise.

23
00:01:29,082 --> 00:01:34,491
The standard measure reads like this: minus one hundred ten dBc per hertz, at ten

24
00:01:34,491 --> 00:01:35,627
kilohertz offset.

25
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:41,036
Translation: ten kilohertz away from the carrier, each hertz of bandwidth holds a

26
00:01:41,036 --> 00:01:45,043
hundred and ten decibels less power than the carrier itself.

27
00:01:45,043 --> 00:01:46,646
More negative is better.

28
00:01:46,646 --> 00:01:51,655
And one design rule worth keeping: multiply an oscillator up to N times its

29
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:57,131
frequency and its phase noise rises by twenty log N decibels — which is why higher

30
00:01:57,131 --> 00:02:00,270
bands are inherently harder on spectral purity.

31
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,542
Now the receive-side trap: reciprocal mixing.

32
00:02:03,542 --> 00:02:07,931
Your receiver's local oscillator multiplies with everything coming in.

33
00:02:07,931 --> 00:02:13,010
If that oscillator has skirts, a strong off-channel signal — a pager, a broadcast

34
00:02:13,010 --> 00:02:18,151
tower — mixes with the skirts, and its energy lands inside your passband as raised

35
00:02:18,151 --> 00:02:22,478
noise, even though the strong signal itself is nominally out of band.

36
00:02:22,478 --> 00:02:27,556
The symptom reads as "this receiver is deaf near that tower," when the real fault

37
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:29,249
is the oscillator's purity.

38
00:02:29,249 --> 00:02:34,265
A cleaner oscillator — or a band-pass filter that keeps the strong neighbour out

39
00:02:34,265 --> 00:02:35,645
entirely — is the fix.

40
00:02:36,095 --> 00:02:40,577
On transmit, the same wobble corrupts the signal directly: it rotates the

41
00:02:40,577 --> 00:02:45,611
constellation a little from symbol to symbol, inflating the error vector magnitude

42
00:02:45,611 --> 00:02:48,927
and capping how dense a modulation the link can carry.

43
00:02:48,927 --> 00:02:53,961
Phase noise is a ceiling on both ends of a link — and no amount of signal strength

44
00:02:53,961 --> 00:02:54,575
raises it.

45
00:02:55,025 --> 00:02:59,945
For GopherTrunk, phase noise arrives baked into the samples: whatever the SDR's

46
00:02:59,945 --> 00:03:04,740
front end contributed before digitization, no software afterwards can remove.

47
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,356
What the concept gives you is a diagnosis.

48
00:03:07,356 --> 00:03:12,213
A signal that looks strong on the waterfall yet decodes with a stubbornly high

49
00:03:12,213 --> 00:03:17,258
error rate near a powerful neighbour is the classic reciprocal-mixing signature —

50
00:03:17,258 --> 00:03:20,932
it points at the receiver's oscillator, not at the decoder.

51
00:03:20,932 --> 00:03:26,101
GopherTrunk's own issue investigations have met exactly this fingerprint: a capture

52
00:03:26,101 --> 00:03:30,398
whose carrier measured clean while the modulation on it was degraded.

53
00:03:30,398 --> 00:03:35,380
Carrier-clean but modulation-degraded is the signature of front-end phase noise.

54
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:40,434
So: phase noise is cycle-to-cycle jitter that smears a carrier into skirts,

55
00:03:40,434 --> 00:03:45,406
measured in dBc per hertz at an offset; reciprocal mixing lets a strong neighbour

56
00:03:45,406 --> 00:03:50,317
ride those skirts into your channel; and a signal that's strong but won't decode

57
00:03:50,317 --> 00:03:54,062
near a big transmitter is your cue to suspect the oscillator.

58
00:03:54,062 --> 00:03:55,719
Full write-up linked below.

